# AQW.csv

Dataset: atmo-tonga-laps — Hunga Tonga 2022 pressure wave: every passage over seven days at the
  102 US ASOS barometers of seis-tonga (IEM one-minute pressure)
File: data/atmo/tonga-laps/raw/AQW.csv
SHA-256: 3f0ab3e4cad03fd9f34d22a8405062aa8b870eb8bbfa2d194d6bb4d5e07d7042 (also in
  data/atmo/tonga-laps/inputs.lock; check with: cd data/atmo/tonga-laps && sha256sum -c inputs.lock)
In the public repository: no, only this description and the hash: the file may be shared but is kept
  out of git for its size. Get it from fog fetch atmo-tonga-laps, then compare its SHA-256 with the
  one above.
Obtained: the answer of a public web API to the request given.
From: Iowa Environmental Mesonet, ASOS one-minute pressure (pres1), one request per station for
  2022-01-15 to 2022-01-22 (fog fetch atmo-tonga-laps).
What could still be wrong: Station barometers report to 0.01 inHg-class precision and some minutes
  are missing; gaps are skipped, not filled.
Collected: 2026-11-21
Licence: other — Iowa Environmental Mesonet (Iowa State University) archive of NWS/FAA ASOS
  one-minute data: open, no account; credit IEM.
Sources:
- Iowa Environmental Mesonet, ASOS one-minute data (pres1), 2022-01-15 to 2022-01-22
  <https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/request/asos/1min.phtml>
- R. S. Matoza et al., Atmospheric waves and global seismoacoustic observations of the January 2022
  Hunga eruption, Tonga, Science 377 (2022) 95-100 (four outbound and three antipodal passages over
  six days) <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abo7063>
Evidence class (rule 10): measure
What would vindicate the flat model (rule 5): Laps that change with the station's position, as any
  echo would make them, or no regular passages after the first two.
Built by: fog build atmo-tonga-laps
Generated files:
- src/data/presets/atmo-tonga-laps.js
Code:
- tools/internal/build/tongalaps
- src/tabs/atmo/lambpool.js
Shown on the page:
- #tab-atmo/lp-h1
Notes: Same stations as seis-tonga (its raw station lists choose them). Picks use no Earth model:
  each passage is the largest band-passed peak 6 to 30 h after the previous one. A lap is read at
  one station, so neither the position nor the explosion time enters.

Written by `fog build explain` from data/atmo/tonga-laps/dataset.json and data/atmo/tonga-laps/inputs.lock: edit those, not this file.
